UPDATE lesson SET content_json=(content_json-'text') || jsonb_build_object('format','markdown','markdown','## Rock is a starting surface, not a finished soil

**Big question:** how can a surface that appears empty begin to support living communities?

Think back to the patch with exposed rock. A plant needs more than a place on a map: it needs suitable physical conditions, access to water and nutrients, and a way to arrive. Fresh volcanic material can provide a starting surface for ecological development, but "volcanic" does not mean that a deep, fertile soil appears immediately. Soil development involves changes in both mineral material and organic matter over time.

Physical weathering breaks material into smaller pieces. Water, temperature changes and other environmental processes can contribute to this breakdown. Cracks and hollows can trap fine material carried by wind or water. As organisms become established, their remains add organic material. These processes interact; they do not form a simple conveyor belt that moves every surface through identical stages at identical speeds.

1. **Material changes:** rock weathers and fine particles accumulate.
2. **Organisms arrive:** suitable microsites provide opportunities to establish.
3. **Conditions change again:** living organisms and organic matter influence their surroundings.

> [!TIP]
> Treat this list as interacting processes, not a fixed timetable. The order and speed can vary.

## Pioneers and small opportunities

The term pioneer describes organisms that can become established early in a developing community. It is a description of their role in a particular setting, not a promise that one named species must always arrive first. Lichens, mosses and other organisms can be useful examples when learning about colonisation of volcanic surfaces. Which organisms actually establish depends on local conditions and on which propagules reach a suitable place.

A microsite is a small location whose conditions differ from its surroundings. A sheltered crack may retain fine material or moisture differently from an exposed ridge of rock. A seed does not experience the average conditions of an entire mountain; it experiences conditions where it lands. This is why a surface that looks almost empty from a distance can still contain small patches of life when studied carefully.

As vegetation develops, it can alter its surroundings. Plant litter contributes organic matter, roots occupy cracks, and the ground beneath vegetation can differ from nearby exposed ground. Those changes may make some conditions more suitable for other organisms, although competition can also increase. Ecological development includes interactions and setbacks, not only a one-directional increase in the number of plants.

## Work through a colonisation puzzle

Allow about two minutes for this invented comparison. Two small areas of the same exposed rock surface are equally old. Area A is a wind-exposed ridge. Area B is a sheltered crack containing fine material. After a season, you observe more seedlings in B. Write two explanations that could fit the observation. Then decide what you would measure to distinguish between them.

- [ ] Suggest two explanations for the different seedling counts.
- [ ] Choose a measurement for each explanation.

::: reveal Explore two possible explanations

One explanation is that the crack retains moisture more effectively. Another is that it collects more seeds or fine material. Both could be operating, and neither is proved by the seedling count alone. Comparing moisture and the arrival of seeds would address different parts of the explanation. Repeating the comparison at several cracks and ridges would make it less dependent on one unusual location. These are examples of testable ideas, not measurements from an actual Etna study.

:::

## Avoid the automatic-timetable mistake

It is tempting to turn succession into a fixed schedule: bare rock in year one, a certain plant in year ten, and a forest in year one hundred. That is not the rule taught here. Conditions differ, disturbances recur and chance arrival can matter. An older exposed patch can remain sparsely vegetated, while a protected microsite on another surface can support plants earlier. Surface age is one variable among several.

Replace ~~one guaranteed timetable~~ with **an explanation that accounts for local conditions**.

For the quiz, remember the relationships. Rock breakdown and accumulated organic matter contribute to soil development. Microsites can differ in moisture, shelter and trapped material. Early colonisers exploit suitable conditions, but no universal sequence or timetable is guaranteed. In the next lesson you will extend this reasoning from a small crack to comparisons across a mountain slope.')
      WHERE id='b1e45468-3281-c0de-8049-40698b6eb982' AND content_json->'demo'='true'::jsonb
        AND NOT (content_json ? 'markdown')
        AND md5(replace(content_json->>'text',chr(13)||chr(10),chr(10)))='957b01f0d92ad8d40a665d7c9bcf3efd';
        WHERE NOT (lesson.content_json ? 'markdown') AND (lesson.content_json->>'text'='Seeded learning history. The lesson player is not implemented.'
           OR lesson.content_json->>'text'='Demo lesson pending optional learning seed.');
UPDATE lesson SET content_json=(content_json-'text') || jsonb_build_object('format','markdown','markdown','## Elevation is part of the explanation

Etna''s living landscape varies with more than the age of its volcanic surfaces. Elevation and the direction a slope faces can influence the conditions found there. The official park description connects vegetation patterns with differences in substrate, temperature, precipitation, altitude and exposure. Those factors help explain why one mountain can contain contrasting habitats rather than a single uniform plant community.

Avoid treating elevation as a magic label. Saying "this plot is higher" describes its position. An ecological explanation asks what changes for organisms at that position. Temperature, moisture and exposure are possible parts of the answer. Likewise, two slopes at similar elevations can differ in their exposure to sun and wind. A useful explanation connects a location to the conditions experienced by living things.

## Make a fair comparison

Suppose you want to test whether a particular kind of sheltered microsite supports more seedlings than exposed ground. If all your sheltered observations come from low slopes and all your exposed observations come from much higher slopes, the comparison mixes shelter with elevation. You would not know which difference contributed to the result. This is a confounding problem: more than one changing factor could explain the pattern.

A stronger design compares sheltered and exposed microsites under otherwise similar conditions, then repeats that comparison in several places. Record the features that might matter instead of selecting only the plots that fit your expectation. Repeated observations cannot remove every uncertainty, but they make your reasoning more transparent and help you recognise exceptions.

| Compare | Keep the method consistent |
| --- | --- |
| Sheltered and exposed microsites | Similar elevation band |
| Several plots, not just one pair | Same counting method |
| Expected and unexpected results | Record both |

### An example notebook format

Use a short, consistent record rather than a different description for every plot:

```text
Plot:
Elevation band:
Sheltered or exposed:
Seedlings counted:
Other observations:
What is still uncertain:
```

## Observation, inference and confidence

Imagine that three sheltered plots contain more seedlings than three exposed plots. That is a pattern in your small sample. It supports further investigation of shelter, but it is not proof that shelter alone controls all vegetation on Etna. The observations could reflect differences in seed arrival, surface history or other conditions you have not measured. The appropriate conclusion should be narrower than the most exciting story you can tell.

Good scientific notes also preserve unexpected results. If one sheltered plot has no seedlings, do not remove it simply because it makes the pattern less tidy. Ask whether it reveals a useful difference, a measurement issue or normal variation. An exception can help you identify the limits of an explanation. "I do not yet know" is a useful statement when it is paired with a clear next question.

## Try a better study plan

Spend about two minutes improving this fictional proposal: "I will count seedlings once in a sheltered low-altitude plot and once on an exposed high-altitude plot. If the counts differ, I will conclude that shelter caused the difference." Identify the confounded factors and write one practical improvement to the comparison. You are designing a desk-based example, not planning an actual visit.

- [ ] Identify which two factors change together.
- [ ] Improve the comparison without discarding inconvenient results.

::: reveal Check your study design

A better proposal would compare several sheltered and exposed plots within a comparable elevation band, record their surface histories where known, and use the same counting method in each plot. Shelter and elevation would no longer change together by design. The result would still describe your sampled plots and conditions, not every part of the mountain or every future year.

:::

For the quiz, focus on fair comparisons. More than one environmental factor can change at once. Repetition and consistent methods strengthen an observation, while selective removal of inconvenient results weakens it. A correlation is a pattern that needs interpretation; it does not automatically identify a single cause.')
