INSERT INTO answer_option(id,question_id,answer_text,is_correct,position) VALUES ('ca3827e3-88cb-6da4-5e4c-8731cd001757','39a135de-7e63-d7cb-7dc2-1c409fd7a260','The topic is exclusively computer hardware.','false','2') ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO lesson(id,course_version_id,position,title,content_json) VALUES ('080b1a5a-059c-a91b-c245-127d2264d94c','3eb5ba6a-3b66-eae6-130a-2e97d02e7cf2','0','Read a volcanic landscape','{"demo":true,"format":"markdown","markdown":"## A mountain made of many events\n\n**Mount Etna** stands on the eastern side of **Sicily, Italy**. It is an *active stratovolcano*: a volcanic mountain built through many eruptions rather than one single event. A landscape photograph can make it look like one enormous cone, but its slopes contain different surfaces, landforms and histories. Summit craters, smaller cones and lava flows are parts of that larger system. The World Heritage designation recognises an outstanding record of ongoing geological processes, not a mountain whose story has finished.\n\nStart with a useful distinction. Magma is molten rock below the surface; once that material erupts at the surface, it is called lava. After lava cools, the resulting rock can remain in the landscape long after a particular eruption has ended. A dark surface is therefore evidence to investigate, not proof that the material is currently hot. The appearance of a surface and the timing of an eruption are different kinds of information.\n\n> [!NOTE]\n> **Magma** is below the surface. **Lava** has erupted at the surface. The same material gets a different name when its setting changes.\n\n## Read the landscape as a patchwork\n\nImagine looking at a hillside from a fixed viewpoint. One area is rough, dark rock with little visible plant cover. Another has scattered vegetation. A third supports established trees. Instead of asking whether the whole mountain is young or old, ask what is different about these patches. The age of a surface, its exposure, its access to water and its history of disturbance can differ over quite short distances.\n\nThis way of thinking matters for ecology. A new eruption does not necessarily replace every habitat on a mountain. Older patches can remain next to recently disturbed ground. Their edges and connections matter because organisms and material can move between places. A single label such as \"volcanic ecosystem\" is useful, but it can hide the variation that organisms actually experience.\n\nSeparate three statements as you look. \"I see little vegetation\" is an observation. \"The surface may be relatively recent\" is an interpretation. \"An eruption map dates this flow\" is supporting evidence from another source. Keeping these statements separate makes it easier to revise an explanation when new information appears. It also prevents a confident-sounding guess from becoming a fact in your notes.\n\n| Kind of statement | Example |\n| --- | --- |\n| Observation | I see little vegetation. |\n| Interpretation | The surface may be relatively recent. |\n| Supporting evidence | A dated eruption map identifies this flow. |\n\n## A small map-reading exercise\n\nSpend about two minutes on this fictional field notebook. Patch A has exposed rock and almost no visible soil. Patch B has scattered plants growing in cracks. Patch C has trees and a deeper layer of leaf litter. All three patches are on the same imaginary slope. Which patch would you investigate first if your question were \"How does vegetation become established on bare surfaces?\" Write one sentence choosing a patch, then a second sentence explaining what additional information you would need.\n\n- [ ] Choose the patch you would investigate first.\n- [ ] Write down one observation without adding an explanation.\n- [ ] Name one extra piece of evidence you would need.\n\n::: reveal Compare your explanation\n\nA reasonable starting point is Patch A, because it represents the least vegetated surface in this description. That does not establish its age or prove that it will develop into Patch C. You would still want information about the surface''s history, its moisture and exposure, and the organisms available nearby. A different research question could justify choosing Patch B or C instead. The quality of the explanation matters more than guessing a universal correct location.\n\n:::\n\n## What to carry into the quiz\n\nEtna is in Sicily, Italy. Its landscape records repeated volcanic activity and contains different kinds of volcanic landform. A stratovolcano is built through many events. When studying the living landscape, think in patches and distinguish an observation from an explanation. In the next lesson you will follow the conditions that allow life to establish itself on exposed rock, without assuming that every patch follows the same timetable.\n\nFor more context, explore [UNESCO''s Mount Etna description](https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1427/).","estimatedMinutes":6}') ON CONFLICT(id) DO UPDATE SET title=excluded.title,content_json=excluded.content_json
